# Manuscript PDF page 196

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ




I took the lid off the case.
    That was the part of the plan I hadn’t written on the sheet. Abuela
had said it on the phone, in a voice like a closing door. She answers who‐
ever asks truly. So you ask first. Vale wanted La Madre to answer his note.
Fine. I would ask her to answer something else.
    I folded back the cloth.
    The heat came up against my face like air from a bread oven. I put
my left palm flat against the dark stone, not the coin, her, and I didn’t
ask for help. I’d learned that. I asked a plainer question.
    Who’s out there?
    The Garden changed.
    A thread of gold ran across the gray, thin as a hair, from my hand to
the edge of the screen. A single line that didn’t belong to the count.
And through the soles of my feet, in a pocket of the room I hadn’t been
listening to, I heard something I recognized: a small, ragged, human
noise. A cough. A child’s sneeze. A toddler laughing at nothing.
    The room was still alive in there. Under the count, each person was
a drum with a loose head. I could hear them holding on.
    Vale’s head turned.
    He was ten yards away, at the lectern, in a pool of warm light, and
he looked straight across the hall at the open case. His face did the
small bright thing again, a man seeing a star.
    “Mr. Cabral,” he said, over the microphone, gently, in the middle of
the count. “Our guest artist has brought something precious to share.”
    Heads turned. Eleven hundred heads, softly, the way heads turn in a
church.
    “Would you bring it to the platform, please?”
    I looked at him across the carpet.
    “No, gracias,” I said.
    I hadn’t used the microphone. I didn’t need to. In a room that quiet,
forty feet carries.
    Somebody in the back laughed.




